# In the platform's component rather than main/Kconfig.projbuild, so that the
# firmware and the on-target test app (apps/firmware_esp32/test_app/), which
# link this component but not main/, see the same symbols -- as
# components/hac_drivers/Kconfig does for HAC_BSEC.

menu "Home Air Monitor: Wi-Fi"

    config HAC_WIFI_SSID
        string "Wi-Fi network name (SSID)"
        default ""
        help
            Phase 4e (docs/adr/0032-wifi-on-the-board.md). The network the
            board's station joins (platform/esp32_t5s3/adp_net.c), 1 to 32
            octets. Empty -- the default -- means no network: the firmware
            never starts Wi-Fi and runs as before, and the test app reports
            the port_net contract as IGNORED.

            Never set it here or in sdkconfig.defaults. It belongs in the
            gitignored overlay apps/firmware_esp32/sdkconfig.credentials
            (copied from sdkconfig.credentials.example), which every build of
            the firmware and the test app layers last over its defaults, and
            which survives a deleted sdkconfig. An overlay reaches only a
            fresh sdkconfig: after changing it, delete the build's sdkconfig
            and run idf.py reconfigure -- the build refuses to go on while
            the two disagree (cmake/credentials_idf.cmake).

            An image built with it holds it in plain text: never attach one
            to an issue or a pull request.

    config HAC_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "Wi-Fi password"
        default ""
        help
            The network's WPA2/WPA3 password, 8 to 64 characters, or empty
            for an open network. Set beside HAC_WIFI_SSID in
            sdkconfig.credentials, never anywhere committed. Handed to
            ESP-IDF's driver in RAM only and never logged; the boot log gives
            its length.

endmenu

menu "Home Air Monitor: MQTT"

    config HAC_MQTT_BROKER_URI
        string "MQTT broker (mqtt://<host>[:<port>])"
        default ""
        help
            Phase 4g (docs/adr/0033-mqtt-on-the-board.md). The broker the
            board reports to (platform/esp32_t5s3/adp_mqtt.c), plain MQTT on
            the local network: mqtt://192.168.1.10:1883, or a name the
            router's DNS resolves (lwIP does not do mDNS: not .local). No
            TLS -- mqtts:// is refused -- and no user or password in the URI:
            they have symbols of their own. Empty -- the default -- means no
            MQTT: the firmware runs as before, Wi-Fi and SNTP included, and
            the test app reports the port_telemetry contract as IGNORED.
            Needs HAC_WIFI_SSID.

            Set it in the gitignored overlay
            apps/firmware_esp32/sdkconfig.credentials, beside the Wi-Fi
            network, never here or in sdkconfig.defaults: it says where the
            owner's network has its broker. An overlay reaches only a fresh
            sdkconfig (cmake/credentials_idf.cmake).

    config HAC_MQTT_USER
        string "MQTT user"
        default ""
        help
            The user the board logs in as, or empty for an anonymous broker.
            In sdkconfig.credentials, never anywhere committed.

    config HAC_MQTT_PASSWORD
        string "MQTT password"
        default ""
        help
            The user's password, or empty. Sent only with a user name (MQTT
            3.1.1, 3.1.2.9), in clear text over the LAN: no TLS (ADR 0033).
            In sdkconfig.credentials, never anywhere committed; never logged
            -- the boot log gives its length. An image built with it holds it
            in plain text: never attach one to an issue or a pull request.

    config HAC_MQTT_TOPIC_PREFIX
        string "MQTT topic prefix"
        default "homeair"
        help
            The first levels of the device's topics, <prefix>/<device
            id>/state and .../status (core/reporting/topics.h): one or more
            levels of [A-Za-z0-9_-], separated by single '/', at most 48
            characters -- an invalid one leaves MQTT off, and the boot log
            says why. The device id is the station MAC's, hac- and twelve
            hex digits. "homeair", the default, is requirements 10.1's
            example and the simulator's; a household with its own prefix sets
            it in sdkconfig.credentials with the broker.

endmenu
